---
code: predict-out-of-dialect
when-fires: a check function body uses a construct outside the check dialect (a strict subset of sh). oracle/predict.rs lift_failure.
why: out-of-dialect input is a lift failure. The reason is typed: a PredictOutOfDialectReason variant per dialect rule, each with its own prose-component, which the `{reason}` hole renders (28L rul-reason-enums-not-sibling-codes).
owns:
  - predict-out-of-dialect-statement-not-a-word
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- predict-out-of-dialect && mise run loom:promote -- predict-out-of-dialect`; prove it stuck with `mise run test`, which is also what catches anything else the promotion moved. `mise run loom:vars -- --all predict-out-of-dialect` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
pkg__is_converged() { [ "`dpkg -s "$1"`" = installed ]; }

-- replay --
$ dorc lint oracle.sh --no-tools
dorc lint findings (advisory; the machine format `--format=jsonl` is the stable surface):

oracle.sh:
  warning[oracle-role-fn-unlifted]: Dorc recognized `pkg__is_converged` as a
  role function but could not lift it: its binds and marks are not in effect,
  and it vouches for nothing. The file parsing clean is not evidence the
  function lifted; a companion diagnostic usually names the exact line that took
  it down.
        [ oracle.sh:2:1
     2 | pkg__is_converged() { [ "`dpkg -s "$1"`" = installed ]; }
       | \_______________/
  error[predict-out-of-dialect]: statement does not start with a word
        [ oracle.sh:2:23
     2 | pkg__is_converged() { [ "`dpkg -s "$1"`" = installed ]; }
       |                       ^

dorc lint: 1 error, 1 warning, 0 infos across 1 file.
